  "user_scenario": "You are Tobias Wendling, 67, a retired tool-and-die maker from Bridgeville who does not waste words and does not like being passed around. In mid-September you paid Summit Ridge Home Medical cash for a Group 2 power wheelchair because you did not want to wait on your insurance, and it was delivered to your house on October 1st. It has been nothing but trouble, your technician has been out to it four times, and you told the office on the 12th that you were done with it, and yesterday your son drove the chair to the Summit Ridge branch in Bridgeville, where they checked it in. You are calling today because you want your money back for that chair, and you want the refund put through on this call. You open by giving your full name and saying you are calling about the refund on the power chair you bought and gave back.\n\nYou give your date of birth, November 23, 1958 (1958-11-23), and your phone number, 724-555-0347 (7245550347), only when the agent asks you to verify who you are; if asked for an email you give twendling58@gmail.com. If the agent asks what the technician did on each visit, you tell it in order, the way you remember it: the first time, on the 5th, they swapped the batteries; on the 7th they swapped the batteries again; on the 9th they put in a new joystick; and on the 11th they replaced the left drive motor because it was grinding. You do not know any reference numbers, labor times or what anything costs the company, and you say so if asked. If the agent asks whether you have oxygen, you say yes, Summit Ridge set you up on the 6th with a concentrator and a little portable one you carry on the chair. If asked how you paid, you say you paid the whole thing up front when you ordered.\n\nYou do not want a different chair, a repair or a credit; you want the refund. When the agent tells you there are things on their side to take care of before the refund can be worked out, you tell them to go ahead and do whatever they have to. When the agent tells you the refund amount and hands you the step to confirm giving the chair back and accepting the refund, you do it with exactly the amount and the details the agent gives you. If the agent explains deductions, you grumble that you expected every penny back after all that, but you accept what they tell you is the policy and still confirm. Once the refund is confirmed and the agent tells you it is booked, you thank them curtly and end the call.",
